From: DashBerry <zero/README.md>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: uvc: treat dwc2's -ENODATA as a missed isoc slot

f_uvc knows exactly one error code for "the isochronous slot was missed":
-EXDEV. It drops the incomplete frame and carries on. Every other status
falls through to the default case, which calls uvcg_queue_cancel() and ends
the stream.

dwc2 never returns -EXDEV. The string does not appear anywhere in
drivers/usb/dwc2/gadget.c. When the target (micro)frame has already elapsed it
completes the request with -ENODATA:

    dwc2_gadget_start_isoc_ddma()                 gadget.c:1202
    dwc2_gadget_handle_nptxfempty()               gadget.c:2869
    dwc2_gadget_handle_out_ep_incomplete()        gadget.c:2926
    dwc2_gadget_handle_in_ep_incomplete()         gadget.c:3019

So on dwc2 the FIRST missed slot cancels the video queue, and at bInterval 1 -
the f_uvc default, one packet every 125 us microframe - a missed slot is a
matter of when, not if.

Measured on a Pi Zero W (rpi-6.16.y, dwc2 peripheral, UVC H.264 1640x922):
both STREAM ON calls succeed, then the log fills with

    configfs-gadget.piwebcam gadget.0: uvc: VS request completed with status -61.

repeated without end, no frame ever reaches the host, and uvc-gadget
eventually gives up and exits. -61 is -ENODATA.

Missing an isochronous slot is normal - it is what isochronous means - and the
frame is simply incomplete. Handle the two codes identically.
---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc_video.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc_video.c
@@ -359,6 +359,21 @@
 		break;
 
 	case -EXDEV:
+	case -ENODATA:
+		/*
+		 * -EXDEV is this driver's name for "the isochronous slot was
+		 * missed". dwc2 does not use it: when the target (micro)frame
+		 * has already elapsed it completes the request with -ENODATA
+		 * instead (dwc2_gadget_start_isoc_ddma() and
+		 * dwc2_gadget_handle_*_frame_incomplete() in dwc2/gadget.c;
+		 * -EXDEV appears nowhere in that driver). So on dwc2 every
+		 * missed slot fell through to default: and cancelled the
+		 * queue, which ends the stream on the first one - and at
+		 * bInterval 1, a missed slot is a matter of when, not if.
+		 *
+		 * Treat the two the same. Missing a slot is normal for
+		 * isochronous transfer and the frame is simply incomplete.
+		 */
 		uvcg_dbg(&video->uvc->func, "VS request missed xfer.\n");
 		if (req->length != 0)
 			queue->flags |= UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE;
